<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Sydney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, founded in 1850, is the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Its main campus at Camperdown in Sydney is known for its Gothic Revival quadrangle. Its archaeologists have led long-running excavations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, among them the excavations at Pella in Jordan, carried out since 1979, and at Zagora on the Greek island of Andros.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-sydney.jpg" alt="University of Sydney" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KittySaturn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Sydney_Main_Quadrangle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1848 two members of the Legislative Council, William Wentworth, an alumnus of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and Sir Charles Nicholson, a graduate of the University of Edinburgh Medical School, proposed expanding the existing Sydney College into a university. Wentworth argued that it would give the child of every class the chance to become great and useful in the destinies of his country, and that a state secular university was imperative for a society aspiring to self-government. The university was founded in 1850. It was one of the world's first universities to admit students solely on academic merit, and it opened its doors to women on the same basis as men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Grant of Arms was made by the College of Arms in 1857, with the motto Sidere mens eadem mutato. The arms show an open book on a blue cross between four eight-pointed stars, with a lion above. Eight-pointed stars were unusual in arms at the time, though they had been used unofficially as emblems of New South Wales since the 1820s. Extension lectures began in 1886, 36 years after the founding, making the university's continuing education programme the longest running in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><p>The university began teaching for the PhD research degree in 1947 after discussing it from 1944. The first PhD was awarded on 28 April 1951 to William H. Wittrick of the Faculty of Engineering, and the next two on 2 May 1951 to Eleanora C. Gyarfas and George F. Humphrey of the Faculty of Science. The New England University College, founded as part of the university in 1938, separated in 1954 to become the University of New England. The Sydney Conservatorium of Music became a faculty of the university in the 1990s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The main campus lies across the inner-city suburbs of Camperdown and Darlington and is noted for its architecture and quadrangle. Five denominational colleges, one Jewish college and one women's college are affiliated with the university, and unlike some residential colleges elsewhere they are not tied to any discipline. The University Library, with 11 individual libraries, holds rare items including a first edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>'s Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica. The University of Sydney Union is the oldest and largest university union in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><p>The university has educated eight Australian prime ministers, two governors-general, 13 premiers of New South Wales and 26 justices of the High Court. Since 2000 the Dr Charles Perkins Oration has honoured its first Aboriginal graduate, Charlie Perkins. The Chau Chak Wing Museum, opened in November 2020, houses the Nicholson Collection of antiquities, the Macleay Collections and the University Art Collection.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Sydney” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Modern analyses suggest that between 3,000 and 4,300 people lived in the precinct, and in 2015 a <b>University of Sydney</b> team reported a road grid, ponds, mounds and low-density housing there, evidence that it was not used only by a priestly elite.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote><b>University of Sydney</b> excavations since 1979 have uncovered fortifications, a Late Bronze Age governor's residence and a temple.</blockquote><a href="/state/pella">Pella</a></li></ul></section>
